; ..\BSW\CanTp\CanTp.c	  6084    \DESCRIPTION    PduR_CanTpStartOfReception is called with several pointer parameters.
; ..\BSW\CanTp\CanTp.c	  6085                    - the PduInfo pointer (if available; see definition of CanTp_PduRStartOfReception macro) is always NULL.
; ..\BSW\CanTp\CanTp.c	  6086                    - the other parameters are derived from the pTpTxState pointer, which is valid in this context.
; ..\BSW\CanTp\CanTp.c	  6087    \COUNTERMEASURE \N -
; ..\BSW\CanTp\CanTp.c	  6088  
; ..\BSW\CanTp\CanTp.c	  6089  \ID SBSW_CANTP_CANIFTRANSMIT_CALL_TXQUEUE
; ..\BSW\CanTp\CanTp.c	  6090    \DESCRIPTION    CanIf_Transmit is called with parameters that have been read from CanTp_TxQueue[ReadPosition]. It is
; ..\BSW\CanTp\CanTp.c	  6091                    checked before that no invalid handle is stored at the read position, so the data which is read
; ..\BSW\CanTp\CanTp.c	  6092                    from the queue can assumed to be valid. Out of bound read accesses are prevented by using a bit mask
; ..\BSW\CanTp\CanTp.c	  6093                    whenever ReadPos is modified (see also [SBSW_CANTP_TXQUEUE_WRITE_ENTRY].
; ..\BSW\CanTp\CanTp.c	  6094                    The only location in code where a valid queue entry is written is in CanTp_CanIfTransmitFrame. There
; ..\BSW\CanTp\CanTp.c	  6095                    it is filled with the same values which are used for the according CanIf_Transmit call.
; ..\BSW\CanTp\CanTp.c	  6096    \COUNTERMEASURE \N -
; ..\BSW\CanTp\CanTp.c	  6097  
; ..\BSW\CanTp\CanTp.c	  6098  \ID SBSW_CANTP_CANIFTRANSMIT_CALL_TPDATA
